NAME
       mkfs.fat - create a FAT (vfat) file system

SYNOPSIS
       mkfs.fat [-F 12|16|32] [-n LABEL] [-I] [-v] DEVICE
       mkfs.vfat [OPTIONS] DEVICE

DESCRIPTION
       mkfs.fat writes an empty FAT file system, the kind used on USB sticks,
       memory cards and the EFI system partition. Linux mounts it with the
       type vfat. mkfs.vfat and mkdosfs are other names for the same
       program. Everything on the device is lost.

       FAT has no Unix owners or permission bits. Every file on a mounted
       vfat file system shows the same owner and mode, chosen at mount time
       with options such as uid=, gid= and umask= (chmod and chown fail).
       It also has no SELinux labels; use the context= mount option when a
       service needs a particular type.

OPTIONS
       -F 12|16|32
              The FAT size. mkfs.fat picks one from the device size when it
              is not given; 32 is usual for anything larger than 512 MiB.

       -n LABEL
              The volume label, up to 11 characters.

       -I     Allow a whole disk (no partition table) as the device.

       -v     Print more details.

EXAMPLES
       Make a vfat file system on /dev/vdc1 and mount it at /media/usb with
       files owned by student:

           mkfs.vfat -n USBDATA /dev/vdc1
           mkdir -p /media/usb
           mount -o uid=student,gid=student /dev/vdc1 /media/usb

       In /etc/fstab:

           UUID=1A2B-3C4D  /media/usb  vfat  defaults,uid=1000,gid=1000  0 0

SEE ALSO
       mkfs(8), fatlabel(8), mount(8), fstab(5)
